<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>This fictional modern episode addresses the presenting concerns below. [S]</paragraph><list><item>A heavy, dull ache in the right breast for about a year and a firm lump she noticed two months ago. [S]</item><item>Dreads an operation; wants every step explained in advance and to be warned before anything is done. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Frances Burney was born in King&apos;s Lynn, Norfolk, on June 13, 1752. [D]</item><item>Burney&apos;s mother died in 1762, when Burney was about ten. [D]</item><item>Burney married Alexandre d&apos;Arblay, whom she met in 1793, and her only child, Alexander, was born in December 1794, when she was 42. In April 1802 she and her son joined her husband in France, where they lived for about ten years. [D]</item><item>Burney was a novelist, diarist, and playwright. Her first novel, Evelina, was published anonymously in January 1778, and she published four novels between 1778 and 1814. [D]</item><item>In her letter Burney wrote that about August 1810 she began to be troubled by &quot;a small pain in my breast,&quot; heavy rather than acute, which increased week by week. She resisted examination for some months; physicians and surgeons then treated her for months without success, the hardness of the affected spot increased, and her right arm was kept from use for many months. She noted that the breast was not discolored and not much larger than the other. [D]</item></list><paragraph>4 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

